From 8b8903a97e93643f537927763adbe8553d6d05ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: turbopack-wasi build <build@turbopack-wasi.invalid>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 09:11:47 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 27/28] turbo-persistence: read files into memory where mmap is
 unsupported (wasi)

turbo-persistence memory-maps every meta/SST/blob file it reads back via
memmap2, which has no wasm32-wasip1 backend: Mmap::map fails at runtime with
"platform not supported". On wasi that made every snapshot commit fail while
opening the meta file it had just written ("Unable to commit snapshot / Failed
to mmap"), roll back via the write guard, and swallow the error -- so the
on-disk store silently persisted nothing (only CURRENT at seq 0) and no run
ever restored.

Introduce a FileMap abstraction (mmap_helper.rs) that is a real memmap2 map on
targets that support it and an owned heap buffer (read the whole file) on wasi.
Both deref to &[u8], so every read path is unchanged; the madvise hints stay
#[cfg(unix)] and are absent on the owned path. A boxed slice has a stable
address for its lifetime, so the zero-copy ArcBytes/RcBytes from_mmap subslice
invariants hold exactly as for an mmap. Native builds are unchanged.

Verified end-to-end against the cal.com fixture under Node WASI: the store now
persists (1.3GB, CURRENT advances), a second run restores in 2.5s vs 62s cold
(24x), and compaction reads old SSTs + writes new -- no mmap/open/checksum
errors.
